athcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/17/178769437e55f1822723ac4cc090efca62aac6be9e3ad9dc8f7971b8a6196484.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186124309 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](images/88/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 false] [17592186124310 1071 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0b/0b395052cc098a6daeb50037d07223b20fb93163c10c31a432c7fb9c9a7804db.svg)" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186124310 1071 "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](images/0d/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" 13194139534279 17592186045417 false] [17592186124310 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](images/50/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\m