 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417131 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417150 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417155 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417156 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1075 #edb/ref 17592186417164 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1077 "selection" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1078 #edb/ref 17592186417155 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417183 1079 #uuid "ef4b0bd2-0e38-5371-ace1-939725dda131" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417178 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1069 "q-82713" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1072 #uuid "15cdd9a6-5092-51ed-93a5-b2dd11d2ff12" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417184 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417174 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1069 "q-82598" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given below. What is $[x]_{B}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1072 #uuid "2be7f5e5-3b31-5fb7-85df-a4fc0339bd35" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417185 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417180 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1069 "q-36500" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1072 #uuid "3ce9ec8d-1b85-5d4c-b1b5-270c31bca4d5" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417186 1074 "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -8 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417181 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1069 "q-113559" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given below. What is $[x]_{C}?$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1072 #uuid "7fe8ffd2-b435-528e-b48e-7a1540a8e35d" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417187 1074 "Given the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-37 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417183 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1069 "q-113612" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1072 #uuid "e28dac56-d247-5873-8ab6-f1ab402c91a9" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417188 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417176 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1069 "q-82717" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1072 #uuid "e6749d20-907b-5109-a739-bd517d7b2b94" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417189 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -9 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417182 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1069 "q-82703" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1071 "$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is given above. Find $[x]_{C}$ given that $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3}.$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1072 #uuid "e866909b-8838-5c06-a42c-cac8cb9d71e5" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417190 1074 "First, notice that since $x=4{b}_{2}-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C,$ we find the coordinates of $x$ relative to $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417175 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1069 "q-82621" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1071 "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1072 #uuid "ef5b0192-0a59-538e-b7e8-f10e0dd5b78e" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417191 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 16 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 13 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417177 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1069 "q-113597" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ to $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is given below. Find $[x]_{B}$ given that $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1072 #uuid "f0cfdcd8-92d0-5768-aa59-2dc769427d08" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true] [17592186417192 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ from $C$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 6-7\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $x=4{c}_{1}+6{c}_{2},$ we obtain  \n\n$$\n[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534279 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-00337181-d246-5d62-ad84-bc4061bb4096" 17592186417124 "answer-06f91c85-a6af-54d8-84f8-c73027a4b2f6" 17592186417125 "answer-164c778b-5b14-5603-bf90-827e8830f635" 17592186417126 "answer-19c523e9-7f45-5159-8b99-5f289dc80d0e" 17592186417127 "answer-1a297d16-52a8-53eb-9dad-1a87b66f4056" 17592186417128 "answer-229988f7-658e-531e-a75e-4a633a968a80" 17592186417129 "answer-339eb9d7-c3ff-5722-b2a4-62c01e506dfe" 17592186417130 "answer-3665ab07-2fe3-53da-8bd4-219390934492" 17592186417131 "answer-3bc312ec-9a5e-5eb2-8bb9-a8936c73c9ce" 17592186417132 "answer-478f5fce-6266-5067-9a1b-4f2ed288f0d0" 17592186417133 "answer-4c7f10ae-9ffa-596d-a2d2-99ebac94264d" 17592186417134 "answer-4e2646f8-d294-5f71-920f-5fb9383c0ce2" 17592186417135 "answer-51c298fd-e567-5dcc-b831-f04003f2f4db" 17592186417136 "answer-5629c555-bc0f-56b8-91fc-75e26ae8c2ad" 17592186417137 "answer-5c1b7743-c305-5999-a721-23098449b1cb" 17592186417138 "answer-61abc92b-674d-5626-b141-2ec57981f70d" 17592186417139 "answer-663faa0d-6301-5578-b7ea-57233f7c6d5c" 17592186417140 "answer-67ef9947-0abd-58ce-bfaa-502e27646e6a" 17592186417141 "answer-7545326f-2ed3-5b45-b83f-fdce64ffd43b" 17592186417142 "answer-76daf001-db03-5b02-b5ce-d1bdb4a9261e" 17592186417143 "answer-78245600-a1f8-5a21-88bd-e06c505e5fc9" 17592186417144 "answer-7b3fc777-c87e-5494-9361-753fc99b8a92" 17592186417145 "answer-848c95d9-4948-5e5b-b405-b76d8bb0404e" 17592186417146 "answer-85f7a332-11d7-56c1-af54-08f89b7cdbb8" 17592186417147 "answer-86daf74f-004a-52d1-918a-94714d07c50d" 17592186417148 "answer-87c5730a-1432-52b5-aaf3-ec874082be11" 17592186417149 "answer-88490a55-b515-5dba-8dd0-a984dd302a75" 17592186417150 "answer-8c4a3883-bbae-5787-8a87-5357e57022a9" 17592186417151 "answer-8d3b91f8-ceab-5fd2-bfae-9dc1cd0ce79f" 17592186417152 "answer-8ec023e5-6b5c-5e85-8721-6e3a12e6f471" 17592186417153 "answer-9968b0a6-1e90-5008-8fc1-0d8dbdcacf08" 17592186417154 "answer-9eeb8242-a2f3-58a1-9c0d-d97cbe1fcdaa" 17592